  "assembly": "# Advent of Code 2025, day 8 part 1 (Playground)\n#\n# THE PUZZLE\n# The input is a list of junction boxes, each a position X,Y,Z. The 1,000 pairs of boxes that are closest\n# together (straight-line distance) are connected. Boxes that are connected, directly or through other\n# boxes, form a circuit. The answer is the sizes of the three largest circuits multiplied together.\n#\n# THE IDEA\n# 1,000 boxes make 499,500 pairs. A distance needs three squares, and the CPU has no multiply, so\n# working out every distance is far too slow. But the 1,000 closest pairs are all short, and a pair\n# cannot be closer than its boxes are apart in X alone. So:\n#   1. SORT. A list of the boxes' addresses, ORDER, is sorted by X.\n#   2. COLLECT. Take a length T. For each box I look at the boxes Q after it in ORDER, and stop at the\n#      first Q that is T or more away in X: all later ones are too. Drop a pair that is T or more apart\n#      in Y or in Z. For the rest work out D2 = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz, the distance squared, and keep\n#      the pairs with D2 < T*T in a list. If the list has fewer than 1,000 pairs, T was too small:\n#      make it larger (below) and start again. Otherwise the 1,000 closest pairs are all in the list: any pair not in\n#      it is at least T apart, and the list has 1,000 or more that are closer.\n#   3. SELECT. Find the largest number V for which fewer than 1,000 pairs of the list have D2 < V, bit by\n#      bit from the top bit down. Then the pairs with D2 <= V are the 1,000 closest.\n#   4. CONNECT. The order of connecting does not matter for what the circuits are in the end. Each box\n#      has a PARENT, at first itself. Following parents from a box ends at a box that is its own parent:\n#      the ROOT of its circuit. Connecting two boxes: find both roots; if they differ, make one the\n#      parent of the other.\n#   5. COUNT. For every box, add 1 to the SIZE of its root. Keep the three largest sizes; multiply them\n#      by adding.\n# How much larger T gets: twice the length holds about eight times the pairs. So T is doubled, and\n# doubled again while that guess says the list would still be too short. A wrong guess costs time only.\n# Squares come from a table of the squares of 0 to 383, which the program makes first by adding the\n# odd numbers. A difference S is cut into H = S / 256 and L = S mod 256, and\n# S*S = H*H * 65536 + 2*H*L * 256 + L*L, with 2*H*L = (H+L)*(H+L) - H*H - L*L: three squares from the\n# table. Comparing D2, not the distance, avoids square roots: the order is the same.\n#\n# THE WHOLE PROGRAM IN PSEUDO CODE\n#     for every box P: PARENT[P] = P; put P's address in ORDER\n#     make the table of squares\n#     sort ORDER by X\n#     T = 1024\n#     loop:                                                         # COLLECT\n#         empty the list\n#         for every I in ORDER:\n#             for every Q after I in ORDER:\n#                 if x(Q) - x(I) >= T: break                        # sorted: the rest is farther still\n#                 if |y(I) - y(Q)| >= T or |z(I) - z(Q)| >= T: continue\n#                 D2 = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz\n#                 if D2 < T*T: append (I, Q, D2) to the list\n#         if the list has K or more pairs: break\n#         T = T + T, and again while (pairs in the list) * 8 per doubling stays below K\n#     V = 0                                                         # SELECT\n#     for STEP in the powers of two below T*T, largest first:\n#         if (number of pairs in the list with D2 < V + STEP) < K: V = V + STEP\n#     for every pair (I, Q, D2) of the list with D2 <= V:           # CONNECT\n#         RA = ROOT(I); RB = ROOT(Q)\n#         if RA != RB: PARENT[RB] = RA\n#     for every box P: SIZE[ROOT(P)] = SIZE[ROOT(P)] + 1            # COUNT\n#     M1, M2, M3 = the three largest SIZE\n#     ANSWER = M1 * M2 * M3\n#     write ANSWER as 10 decimal digits\n#     stop\n#\n# NUMBERS OF 32 BITS\n# A coordinate, D2, V and the answer are wider than a memory word. Each is two 16-bit words, lower word\n# first, and fits the 32-bit registers whole: get32A v loads both words of v into A, and one sum or sub\n# works on all 32 bits. Loading the two words of one register needs the other register for the address.\n# A variable's upper word has the name of the variable with _H.\n#\n# THE RESULT, when the CPU stops on the Breakpoint at END\n#   0x0100-0x0101  the answer as two 16-bit words, lower first\n#   0x0110-0x0119  the answer as 10 decimal digits, one per word, most significant first\n#\n# THE INPUT (rule 1 of RULES.md), at 0x2000\n# Six words per box: X, Y, Z, each as two 16-bit words, lower word first (99967 = 0x0001_867F is stored\n# as 0x867F; 0x0001). After the last box come two words 0xFFFF. The number of connections to make is\n# the word K below (1000 for the puzzle, 10 for its example).\n# Built-in limits: at most 1,023 boxes; coordinates below 2^31; the K-th closest pair is closer than\n# 32,768 (T may not grow beyond that: the squares must fit 32 bits); COLLECT's last list has at most\n# 8,960 pairs; no two pairs at exactly the distance of the K-th closest (the puzzle would not say which\n# to take; the program would connect both); there are at least K pairs.\n#\n# THE IDIOMS (v is a variable's address, below 0x1000)\n#     getA v / getB v          A or B = the word at v, upper half 0\n#     putA v                   the word at v = A   (this uses up B)\n#     get32A v / get32B v      A or B = the 32-bit number at v and v + 1; loading one register uses up the other\n#     get32AB v w              A = the 32-bit number at v, B = the one at w (A waits on the stack while B is loaded)\n#     put32A v                 the 32-bit number at v and v + 1 = A   (this uses up B)\n#     getAat p / putAat p      the same as getA / putA for the word the pointer p points at\n#     getAoff p k              A = the word at the address (p + k); p is a variable holding an address\n#     copy v w                 w = v\n#     keepcarry v              v = the upper half of A: the carry of the sum just made\n#     if_eq L / if_neq L       compares A with B as they are now\n#     sub; if_carry L          after A - B: taken when A >= B\n# |a - b| is: sub; when that went below 0, flip every bit (neg) and add 1.\n# psh X; ... pop Y moves 16 bits from one register half to another by way of the stack.\n# What each instruction does exactly is in findings/cpu-instructions.md.\n#\n# MEMORY MAP\n#   0x0100-0x017F  variables\n#   0x1000-        the program          0x2000-  the boxes (1,000 and the end mark end at 0x3771)\n#   0x1800-        the subroutines      0x3800-  PARENT and SIZE: for the box at address P, PARENT is the word\n#                                                at P + 0x1800 and SIZE the word at P + 0x1801; all 0 at the start\n#   0x1A00-0x1DFF  ORDER: the addresses of the boxes, sorted by X\n#   0x1E00-        POWERS: 10^9 down to 1, two words each, then 0; BITS at 0x1E40: 2^29 down to 1, then 0, 0;\n#                  GAPS at 0x1E80: the gaps of the sort, then 0\n#   0x5000-0x537F  SQ: the squares of 0 to 383; lower words at 0x5000 + n, upper words at 0x5200 + n\n#   0x5400-0xDFFF  the list: four words per pair (address of I, address of Q, D2 lower word, D2 upper word)\n#   0xFF00         where the CPU starts\n\n# ---- The CPU's instructions as symbols (day 1's table without its _32 names, which the CPU ignores) ----\nsymbol nop 0b0000_0000_0000_0000            # the word 0: stops the CPU (the same word as non)\nsymbol ld  0b0010_0000_0000_0000            # load a register from memory (used through the load macro)\nsymbol lia 0b0011_0000_0000_0000            # lia n: A = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol lib 0b0100_0000_0000_0000            # lib n: B = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol sto 0b0101_0000_0000_0000            # store a register to memory (used through the store macro)\n\nmacro  load  { ld  | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # load X Y: X = the word at the address in Y\nmacro  store { sto | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # store X Y: the word at the address in Y = X\n\nsymbol sum    0b0110_0100_0000_0000         # A = A + B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol sub    0b0110_0010_0000_0000         # A = A - B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol shf    0b0110_0001_0000_0000         # shf | k: A = A shifted left by k (0 to 15)\nsymbol and    0b0110_0000_1000_0000         # A = A and B\nsymbol or     0b0110_0000_0100_0000         # A = A or B\nsymbol xor    0b0110_0000_0010_0000         # A = A xor B\nsymbol neg    0b0110_0000_0001_0000         # A = every bit of A flipped\n\nsymbol psh 0b1010_0000_0000_0000            # psh X: push the 16 bits of X\nsymbol pop 0b1011_0000_0000_0000            # pop X: pop 16 bits into X\n\nsymbol jmp  0b1100_0000_0000_0000           # jmp; L: go to L.  jmp c; L: go to L when c holds\nsymbol call 0b1110_0000_0000_0000           # call; L: run the subroutine at L\nsymbol ret  0b1111_0000_0000_0000           # return from a subroutine\n\n# The registers, as load, store, psh and pop name them\nsymbol rgAH 0b1000_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgA  0b0100_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgBH 0b0010_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgB  0b0001_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgSP 0b0000_1000_0000                # the program counter (leave alone)\nsymbol rgSB 0b0000_0100_0000                # the stack pointer (leave alone)\nsymbol non  0                               # stops the CPU (Breakpoint): the end of the program\n\n# The conditions of jmp\nsymbol zero  0b1000_0000_0000               # the last sum or sub gave 0\nsymbol carry 0b0100_0000_0000               # the last sum carried, or the last sub did not borrow (A >= B)\nsymbol nega  0b0010_0000_0000               # the last sub went below 0 (A < B)\nsymbol eq    0b0001_0000_0000               # A == B right now\nsymbol neq   0b0000_1000_0000               # A != B right now\n\n# ---- Macros for the common operations (explained in the README). A macro cannot use another macro, so\n#      load and store are spelled out here. v: a variable, p: a variable holding an address, L: a label ----\nmacro getA     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                                              # A = v\nmacro getB     { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6) }                                              # B = v\nmacro putA     { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                                             # v = A (uses B)\nmacro getAat   { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                       # A = the word p points at\nmacro putAat   { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6); sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # the word p points at = A (uses B)\nmacro inc      { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib 1; sum; lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }  # v = v + 1 (uses A and B)\nmacro goto     { jmp; a? }                                                                    # go to L\nmacro if_eq    { jmp eq; a? }                                                                 # go to L when A == B\nmacro do       { call; a? }                                                                   # call the subroutine L\nmacro if_carry { jmp carry; a? }                                                              # go to L after a sub when A >= B\nmacro getAoff  { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib b?; sum; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                # A = the word at (p + k) (uses B)\nmacro copy     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib b?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # w = v (uses A and B)\nmacro get32A   { lib | (a? + 1); ld | rgAH | (rgB >> 6); lib a?; ld | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                  # A = the 32 bits at v, v + 1 (uses B)\nmacro get32B   { lia | (a? + 1); ld | rgBH | (rgA >> 6); lia a?; ld | rgB | (rgA >> 6) }                  # B = the 32 bits at v, v + 1 (uses A)\nmacro get32AB  { lib | (a? + 1); ld | rgAH | (rgB >> 6); lib a?; ld | rgA | (rgB >> 6); psh rgAH; psh rgA; lia | (b? + 1); ld | rgBH | (rgA >> 6); lia b?; ld | rgB | (rgA >> 6); pop rgA; pop rgAH }\n# get32AB v w: A = the 32 bits at v, B = the 32 bits at w\nmacro put32A   { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6); lib | (a? + 1); sto | rgAH | (rgB >> 6) }                # the 32 bits at v, v + 1 = A (uses B)\n\nmacro keepcarry { psh rgAH; pop rgB; lia a?; sto | rgB | (rgA >> 6) }                                 # v = the upper half of A (uses A and B)\nmacro if_neq   { jmp neq; a? }                                                                # go to L when A != B\n\n\n# ---- Variables (all below 0x1000, so lia/lib can address them). NAME_H is the upper word of the 32-bit NAME ----\n# The result\npointer ANSWER 0x0100 0\npointer ANSWER_H 0x0101 0\n# K: the number of connections to make (the puzzle's text gives it). TARGET: the length of K pairs in the list, K * 4\npointer K 0x0120 1000\npointer TARGET 0x0121 0\n# T: pairs T or more apart in one direction are dropped. TSQ = T * T\npointer T 0x0122 1024\npointer TSQ 0x0123 0\npointer TSQ_H 0x0124 0x0010\n# P: the address of a box, for the loops over all boxes. PEND: the address after the last box\npointer P 0x0125 0x2000\npointer PEND 0x0126 0\n# ORDER is the list of box addresses that gets sorted by X. OP: where the next address goes. OEND: the address after its last word\npointer OP 0x0127 0x1A00\npointer OEND 0x0128 0\n# For the sort: GAPP the next gap in the table, GAP the gap, IP the word being placed, JP where it may go, GP = JP - GAP\npointer GAPP 0x0129 0x1E80\npointer GAP 0x012A 0\npointer JP 0x012B 0\npointer GP 0x012C 0\n# MOVED: the box address being placed, KEY its X. OTHER: the box address at GP, OTHER_X its X\npointer MOVED 0x012D 0\npointer KEY 0x012E 0\npointer KEY_H 0x012F 0\npointer OTHER 0x0130 0\npointer OTHER_X 0x0131 0\npointer OTHER_X_H 0x0132 0\n# For the table of squares: NEXT_SQ the next square, ODD what the square after it is larger by, LP and HP where its two words go\npointer NEXT_SQ 0x0133 0\npointer NEXT_SQ_H 0x0134 0\npointer ODD 0x0135 1\npointer LP 0x0136 0x5000\npointer HP 0x0137 0x5200\n# IP, QP: the places in ORDER of the two boxes of a pair. I, Q: their addresses. XI, YI, ZI: a copy of I's position\npointer IP 0x0138 0\npointer QP 0x0139 0\npointer I 0x013A 0\npointer Q 0x013B 0\npointer XI 0x013C 0\npointer XI_H 0x013D 0\npointer YI 0x013E 0\npointer YI_H 0x013F 0\npointer ZI 0x0140 0\npointer ZI_H 0x0141 0\n# CJ: a copy of one coordinate of Q. DX, DY, DZ: how far apart the pair is in each direction (below T)\npointer CJ 0x0142 0\npointer CJ_H 0x0143 0\npointer DX 0x0144 0\npointer DY 0x0145 0\npointer DZ 0x0146 0\n# D2: the distance of a pair, squared\npointer D2 0x0147 0\npointer D2_H 0x0148 0\n# For ADD_SQUARE: S the number to square, H = S / 256, L = S mod 256, SH = H * H, SL = L * L, HL = H + L\npointer S 0x0149 0\npointer H 0x014A 0\npointer L 0x014B 0\npointer SH 0x014C 0\npointer SL 0x014D 0\npointer HL 0x014E 0\n# LIST_END: the address after the last pair of the list. E: the address of the pair of the list being looked at\npointer LIST_END 0x014F 0\npointer E 0x0150 0\n# GUESS: the words in the list after a round of COLLECT, then the guess for a larger T\npointer GUESS 0x0151 0\n# For SELECT: V the number being built, STEP the power of two being tried, LIMIT = V + STEP\npointer V 0x0152 0\npointer V_H 0x0153 0\npointer STEP 0x0154 0\npointer STEP_H 0x0155 0\npointer LIMIT 0x0156 0\npointer LIMIT_H 0x0157 0\n# BP: the first power of two SELECT tries: the largest below TSQ (2^19 for T = 1024)\npointer BP 0x0158 0x1E54\n# F: FIND starts at the box at F and leaves the root of its circuit in F. RA: the root of the pair's first box\npointer F 0x0159 0\npointer RA 0x015A 0\n# ADR: an address worked out for a load or a store\npointer ADR 0x015B 0\n# COUNT: the pairs SELECT found below LIMIT\npointer COUNT 0x015C 0\n# M1 >= M2 >= M3: the three largest sizes. SIZE: the size being looked at\npointer M1 0x015D 0\npointer M2 0x015E 0\npointer M3 0x015F 0\npointer SIZE 0x0160 0\n# For the products: PRODUCT = M1 * M2, TIMES counts the additions down\npointer PRODUCT 0x0161 0\npointer PRODUCT_H 0x0162 0\npointer TIMES 0x0163 0\n# For the decimal digits: what is left of the answer, the current power of ten, how often it fitted\npointer REST 0x0164 0\npointer REST_H 0x0165 0\npointer POWER 0x0166 0\npointer POWER_H 0x0167 0\npointer DIGIT_VALUE 0x0168 0\n# Pointers: DIGIT the next digit to write, POW the next power of ten\npointer DIGIT 0x0169 0x0110\npointer POW 0x016A 0x1E00\n# Constants that do not fit an instruction's place for a number\npointer POINTS 0x016B 0x2000\npointer ORDER 0x016C 0x1A00\npointer LIST 0x016D 0x5400\npointer SQLO 0x016E 0x5000\npointer SQHI 0x016F 0x5200\npointer SQ_END 0x0170 0x5180\npointer TO_PARENT 0x0171 0x1800\npointer END_MARK 0x0172 0xFFFF\npointer TO_SIZE 0x0173 0x1801\n\n# ---- The CPU starts at 0xFF00 ----\norigin 0xFF00\ngoto MAIN\n\norigin 0x1000\n@ MAIN\n# ==== go through the boxes once ====\n#     while the box at P is not the end mark:               # P starts at the first box, OP at ORDER\n#         memory[OP] = P; OP = OP + 1                       # ORDER: the addresses of all boxes\n#         PARENT[P] = P\n#         P = P + 6\n#     PEND = P; OEND = OP\n#     TARGET = K * 4\n@ INIT_NEXT\n\tgetAoff P 1; getB END_MARK                                   # A = the upper word of the box's X, B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq INIT_DONE                                              # the end mark: no more boxes\n\tgetA P; putAat OP                                            # memory[OP] = P\n\tinc OP                                                       # OP = OP + 1\n\tgetA P; getB TO_PARENT; sum                                  # A = P + 0x1800, the address of the box's PARENT\n\tgetB P; store rgB rgA                                        # PARENT[P] = P\n\tgetA P; lib 6; sum; putA P                                   # P = P + 6, the next box\n\tgoto INIT_NEXT\n@ INIT_DONE\n\tcopy P PEND                                                  # PEND = the address after the last box\n\tcopy OP OEND                                                 # OEND = the address after the last word of ORDER\n\tgetA K; shf | 2; putA TARGET                                 # TARGET = K * 4: a pair takes four words in the list\n\n# ==== the table of squares: SQ[n] = n * n for n = 0 to 383 ====\n# The next square is the last one plus the next odd number: 0, 0 + 1, 1 + 3, 4 + 5, 9 + 7, ...\n#     NEXT_SQ = 0; ODD = 1\n#     for LP = SQLO, HP = SQHI; LP != SQ_END; LP = LP + 1, HP = HP + 1:\n#         memory[LP] = lower word of NEXT_SQ; memory[HP] = upper word of NEXT_SQ\n#         NEXT_SQ = NEXT_SQ + ODD; ODD = ODD + 2\n@ SQUARES_NEXT\n\tgetA NEXT_SQ; putAat LP                                      # memory[LP] = the lower word of the square\n\tgetA NEXT_SQ_H; putAat HP                                    # memory[HP] = its upper word\n\tget32A NEXT_SQ; getB ODD; sum; put32A NEXT_SQ                # NEXT_SQ = NEXT_SQ + ODD\n\tgetA ODD; lib 2; sum; putA ODD                               # ODD = ODD + 2\n\tinc HP                                                       # HP = HP + 1\n\tinc LP                                                       # LP = LP + 1\n\tgetB SQ_END                                                  # B = the address after the table (A still holds LP)\n\tif_neq SQUARES_NEXT\n\n# ==== sort ORDER by X, smallest first (a Shell sort) ====\n# An insertion sort moves each word left past the words with a larger X, one place at a time. A Shell\n# sort does the same with a GAP: first words 364 places apart are put in order, then 121, 40, 13, 4,\n# and at last 1, when nothing has far to go any more.\n#     for GAP in 364, 121, 40, 13, 4, 1:                    # from the table GAPS, which ends in 0\n#         for IP = ORDER + GAP; IP < OEND; IP = IP + 1:\n#             MOVED = memory[IP]; KEY = x(MOVED)\n#             JP = IP\n#             loop:\n#                 GP = JP - GAP\n#                 if GP < ORDER: break\n#                 OTHER = memory[GP]\n#                 if x(OTHER) <= KEY: break\n#                 memory[JP] = OTHER; JP = GP               # OTHER moves GAP places to the right\n#             memory[JP] = MOVED\n@ SORT_NEXT_GAP\n\tgetAat GAPP; putA GAP                                        # GAP = the next gap of the table\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq SORTED                                                 # the 0 that ends the table\n\tinc GAPP                                                     # GAPP = GAPP + 1\n\tgetA ORDER; getB GAP; sum; putA IP                           # IP = ORDER + GAP\n@ SORT_NEXT_WORD\n\tgetA IP; getB OEND; sub                                      # A = IP - OEND\n\tif_carry SORT_NEXT_GAP                                       # IP >= OEND: this gap is done\n\tgetAat IP; putA MOVED                                        # MOVED = memory[IP]\n\tgetAat MOVED; putA KEY                                       # KEY = x(MOVED); lower word\n\tgetAoff MOVED 1; putA KEY_H                                  # upper word\n\tcopy IP JP                                                   # JP = IP\n@ SORT_LEFT\n\tgetA JP; getB GAP; sub; putA GP                              # GP = JP - GAP\n\tgetB ORDER; sub                                              # A = GP - ORDER\n\tif_carry SORT_COMPARE                                        # GP >= ORDER: there is a word GAP places to the left\n\tgoto SORT_PLACE\n@ SORT_COMPARE\n\tgetAat GP; putA OTHER                                        # OTHER = memory[GP]\n\tgetAat OTHER; putA OTHER_X                                   # OTHER_X = x(OTHER); lower word\n\tgetAoff OTHER 1; putA OTHER_X_H                              # upper word\n\tget32AB KEY OTHER_X; sub                                     # A = KEY - x(OTHER)\n\tif_carry SORT_PLACE                                          # x(OTHER) <= KEY: MOVED belongs at JP\n\tgetA OTHER; putAat JP                                        # memory[JP] = OTHER\n\tcopy GP JP                                                   # JP = GP\n\tgoto SORT_LEFT\n@ SORT_PLACE\n\tgetA MOVED; putAat JP                                        # memory[JP] = MOVED\n\tinc IP                                                       # IP = IP + 1\n\tgoto SORT_NEXT_WORD\n@ SORTED\n\n# ==== COLLECT: the pairs closer than T ====\n#     LIST_END = LIST                                       # the list is empty\n#     for IP = ORDER; IP != OEND; IP = IP + 1:              # I: every box, from the smallest X upwards\n#         I = memory[IP]; XI, YI, ZI = the position of I\n#         for QP = IP + 1; QP != OEND; QP = QP + 1:         # Q: the boxes after I in ORDER\n#             Q = memory[QP]\n#             (the pair I, Q: below)\n#     if LIST_END - LIST < TARGET: GROW (below); start again\n@ COLLECT\n\tcopy LIST LIST_END                                           # the list is empty\n\tcopy ORDER IP                                                # IP = the first place of ORDER\n@ NEXT_I\n\tgetA IP; getB OEND                                           # A = IP, B = OEND\n\tif_eq COLLECTED                                              # every box has been I\n\tgetAat IP; putA I                                            # I = memory[IP]\n\tgetAat I; putA XI                                            # XI = the X of I; lower word\n\tgetAoff I 1; putA XI_H                                       # upper word\n\tgetAoff I 2; putA YI                                         # YI = the Y of I; lower word\n\tgetAoff I 3; putA YI_H                                       # upper word\n\tgetAoff I 4; putA ZI                                         # ZI = the Z of I; lower word\n\tgetAoff I 5; putA ZI_H                                       # upper word\n\tgetA IP; lib 1; sum; putA QP                                 # QP = IP + 1\n\n# ---- one pair ----\n#     if x(Q) - XI >= T: goto END_OF_I          # ORDER is sorted by X: every later Q is as far or farther\n#     if |y(Q) - YI| >= T: goto NEXT_Q\n#     if |z(Q) - ZI| >= T: goto NEXT_Q\n#     D2 = DX*DX + DY*DY + DZ*DZ\n#     if D2 >= TSQ: goto NEXT_Q\n#     append I, Q, D2 to the list\n@ NEXT_PAIR\n\tgetA QP; getB OEND                                           # A = QP, B = OEND\n\tif_eq END_OF_I                                               # no box is left to pair I with\n\tgetAat QP; putA Q                                            # Q = memory[QP]\n\tgetAoff Q 1; shf | 8; shf | 8                                # A = the upper word of Q's X, moved into the upper half of A\n\tgetB Q; load rgA rgB                                         # the lower half of A = the lower word: A = Q's X\n\tpsh rgAH; psh rgA; get32B XI; pop rgA; pop rgAH              # B = I's X (loading B uses A, so A waits on the stack)\n\tsub                                                          # A = Q's X - I's X, which is not below 0\n\tgetB T; sub                                                  # A = that - T\n\tif_carry END_OF_I                                            # T or more apart in X, and so is every box after Q\n\tsum; putA DX                                                 # DX = the distance in X (A + T again; B still holds T)\n\tgetAoff Q 2; putA CJ                                         # CJ = Q's Y; lower word\n\tgetAoff Q 3; putA CJ_H                                       # upper word\n\tget32AB CJ YI                                                # A = Q's Y, B = I's Y\n\tsub                                                          # A = the difference, 32 bits\n\tif_carry Y_POSITIVE                                          # it did not go below 0\n\tneg; lib 1; sum                                              # below 0: A = -A (flip every bit, add 1)\n@ Y_POSITIVE\n\tgetB T; sub                                                  # A = the distance in this direction - T\n\tif_carry NEXT_Q                                              # T or more apart: this pair is not among the closest\n\tsum; putA DY                                                 # DY = the distance in this direction (A + T again; B still holds T)\n\tgetAoff Q 4; putA CJ                                         # CJ = Q's Z; lower word\n\tgetAoff Q 5; putA CJ_H                                       # upper word\n\tget32AB CJ ZI                                                # A = Q's Z, B = I's Z\n\tsub                                                          # A = the difference, 32 bits\n\tif_carry Z_POSITIVE                                          # it did not go below 0\n\tneg; lib 1; sum                                              # below 0: A = -A (flip every bit, add 1)\n@ Z_POSITIVE\n\tgetB T; sub                                                  # A = the distance in this direction - T\n\tif_carry NEXT_Q                                              # T or more apart: this pair is not among the closest\n\tsum; putA DZ                                                 # DZ = the distance in this direction (A + T again; B still holds T)\n\tlia 0; putA D2; putA D2_H                                    # D2 = 0\n\tcopy DX S; do ADD_SQUARE                                     # D2 = D2 + DX * DX\n\tcopy DY S; do ADD_SQUARE                                     # D2 = D2 + DY * DY\n\tcopy DZ S; do ADD_SQUARE                                     # D2 = D2 + DZ * DZ\n\tget32AB D2 TSQ; sub                                          # A = D2 - TSQ\n\tif_carry NEXT_Q                                              # D2 >= T * T: the pair is T or more apart\n\tgetA I; putAat LIST_END; inc LIST_END                        # append to the list: the address of I\n\tgetA Q; putAat LIST_END; inc LIST_END                        # the address of Q\n\tgetA D2; putAat LIST_END; inc LIST_END                       # the lower word of D2\n\tgetA D2_H; putAat LIST_END; inc LIST_END                     # the upper word of D2\n@ NEXT_Q\n\tinc QP                                                       # QP = QP + 1\n\tgoto NEXT_PAIR\n@ END_OF_I\n\tinc IP                                                       # IP = IP + 1\n\tgoto NEXT_I\n@ COLLECTED\n\tgetA LIST_END; getB LIST; sub; putA GUESS                    # GUESS = LIST_END - LIST, the words in the list\n\tgetB TARGET; sub                                             # A = GUESS - TARGET\n\tif_carry SELECT                                              # K pairs or more: the K closest are all in the list\n\n# ---- T was too small. Twice the length holds about eight times the pairs, so T is doubled until ----\n# ---- that guess reaches TARGET (and once in any case). The guess only saves rounds: COLLECT tests again ----\n#     loop:\n#         T = T + T; TSQ = TSQ * 4; BP = BP - 4\n#         GUESS = GUESS * 8\n#         if GUESS >= TARGET or GUESS == 0: break\n@ GROW\n\tgetA T; shf | 1; putA T                                      # T = T * 2\n\tget32A TSQ; shf | 2; put32A TSQ                              # TSQ = TSQ * 4\n\tgetA BP; lib 4; sub; putA BP                                 # BP = BP - 4: SELECT starts two powers of two higher\n\tgetA GUESS; shf | 3; getB TARGET; sub                        # A = GUESS * 8 - TARGET\n\tif_carry COLLECT                                             # eight times the pairs would be enough: try this T\n\tgetA GUESS; shf | 3; putA GUESS                              # GUESS = GUESS * 8\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq COLLECT                                                # no pair was found: there is nothing to guess from\n\tgoto GROW\n\n# ==== SELECT: V = the D2 of the K-th closest pair ====\n#     V = 0\n#     for STEP in the powers of two from the one at BP down to 1:       # the table BITS ends in 0\n#         LIMIT = V + STEP\n#         COUNT = the number of pairs in the list with D2 < LIMIT\n#         if COUNT < K: V = LIMIT\n# In the end fewer than K pairs have D2 < V, and K or more have D2 < V + 1: V is the K-th smallest D2.\n@ SELECT\n\tlia 0; putA V; putA V_H                                      # V = 0\n@ NEXT_STEP\n\tgetAat BP; putA STEP                                         # STEP = the next power of two; lower word\n\tgetAoff BP 1; putA STEP_H                                    # upper word\n\tget32A STEP; lib 0                                           # A = STEP, B = 0\n\tif_eq SELECTED                                               # the 0 that ends the table\n\tgetA BP; lib 2; sum; putA BP                                 # BP = BP + 2\n\tget32AB V STEP; sum; put32A LIMIT                            # LIMIT = V + STEP\n\tlia 0; putA COUNT                                            # COUNT = 0\n\tcopy LIST E                                                  # E = the first pair of the list\n@ COUNT_NEXT\n\tgetA E; getB LIST_END                                        # A = E, B = LIST_END\n\tif_eq COUNTED                                                # the end of the list\n\tgetAoff E 2; putA D2                                         # D2 = the pair's D2; lower word\n\tgetAoff E 3; putA D2_H                                       # upper word\n\tget32AB D2 LIMIT; sub                                        # A = D2 - LIMIT\n\tif_carry COUNT_STEP                                          # D2 >= LIMIT: not counted\n\tinc COUNT                                                    # COUNT = COUNT + 1\n@ COUNT_STEP\n\tgetA E; lib 4; sum; putA E                                   # E = E + 4, the next pair\n\tgoto COUNT_NEXT\n@ COUNTED\n\tgetA COUNT; getB K; sub                                      # A = COUNT - K\n\tif_carry NEXT_STEP                                           # K or more pairs are below LIMIT: V stays\n\tcopy LIMIT V; copy LIMIT_H V_H                               # fewer than K: V = LIMIT\n\tgoto NEXT_STEP\n\n# ==== CONNECT: join the two boxes of every pair with D2 <= V ====\n#     LIMIT = V + 1\n#     for every pair (I, Q, D2) of the list with D2 < LIMIT:\n#         RA = ROOT(I); F = ROOT(Q)\n#         if F != RA: PARENT[F] = RA\n@ SELECTED\n\tget32A V; lib 1; sum; put32A LIMIT                           # LIMIT = V + 1\n\tcopy LIST E                                                  # E = the first pair of the list\n@ CONNECT_NEXT\n\tgetA E; getB LIST_END                                        # A = E, B = LIST_END\n\tif_eq CONNECTED                                              # the end of the list\n\tgetAoff E 2; putA D2                                         # D2 = the pair's D2; lower word\n\tgetAoff E 3; putA D2_H                                       # upper word\n\tget32AB D2 LIMIT; sub                                        # A = D2 - LIMIT\n\tif_carry CONNECT_STEP                                        # D2 > V: not one of the K closest\n\tgetAat E; putA F; do FIND                                    # F = the root of the pair's first box\n\tcopy F RA                                                    # RA = that root\n\tgetAoff E 1; putA F; do FIND                                 # F = the root of the pair's second box\n\tgetA F; getB RA                                              # A = F, B = RA\n\tif_eq CONNECT_STEP                                           # the same root: the boxes are in one circuit already\n\tgetA F; getB TO_PARENT; sum                                  # A = F + 0x1800, the address of F's PARENT\n\tgetB RA; store rgB rgA                                       # PARENT[F] = RA: the two circuits are one\n@ CONNECT_STEP\n\tgetA E; lib 4; sum; putA E                                   # E = E + 4, the next pair\n\tgoto CONNECT_NEXT\n\n# ==== COUNT: the size of every circuit, kept at its root ====\n#     for every box P: F = ROOT(P); SIZE[F] = SIZE[F] + 1\n@ CONNECTED\n\tcopy POINTS P                                                # P = the first box\n@ SIZE_NEXT\n\tgetA P; getB PEND                                            # A = P, B = PEND\n\tif_eq SIZED                                                  # every box is counted\n\tcopy P F; do FIND                                            # F = the root of P's circuit\n\tgetA F; getB TO_SIZE; sum; putA ADR                          # ADR = F + 0x1801, the address of the root's SIZE\n\tgetAat ADR; lib 1; sum; putAat ADR                           # SIZE[F] = SIZE[F] + 1\n\tgetA P; lib 6; sum; putA P                                   # P = P + 6, the next box\n\tgoto SIZE_NEXT\n\n# ==== the three largest sizes ====\n#     for every box P:                                      # a box that is not a root has SIZE 0\n#         if SIZE[P] >= M1: M3 = M2; M2 = M1; M1 = SIZE[P]\n#         else if SIZE[P] >= M2: M3 = M2; M2 = SIZE[P]\n#         else if SIZE[P] >= M3: M3 = SIZE[P]\n@ SIZED\n\tcopy POINTS P                                                # P = the first box\n@ TOP_NEXT\n\tgetA P; getB PEND                                            # A = P, B = PEND\n\tif_eq TOP_DONE                                               # every box has been looked at\n\tgetA P; getB TO_SIZE; sum; load rgA rgA; putA SIZE           # SIZE = SIZE[P]\n\tgetB M1; sub                                                 # A = SIZE - M1 (A still holds SIZE)\n\tif_carry NEW_FIRST                                           # SIZE >= M1\n\tgetA SIZE; getB M2; sub                                      # A = SIZE - M2\n\tif_carry NEW_SECOND                                          # SIZE >= M2\n\tgetA SIZE; getB M3; sub                                      # A = SIZE - M3\n\tif_carry NEW_THIRD                                           # SIZE >= M3\n@ TOP_STEP\n\tgetA P; lib 6; sum; putA P                                   # P = P + 6, the next box\n\tgoto TOP_NEXT\n@ NEW_FIRST\n\tcopy M2 M3; copy M1 M2; copy SIZE M1                         # M3 = M2; M2 = M1; M1 = SIZE\n\tgoto TOP_STEP\n@ NEW_SECOND\n\tcopy M2 M3; copy SIZE M2                                     # M3 = M2; M2 = SIZE\n\tgoto TOP_STEP\n@ NEW_THIRD\n\tcopy SIZE M3                                                 # M3 = SIZE\n\tgoto TOP_STEP\n\n# ==== ANSWER = M1 * M2 * M3, by adding ====\n#     PRODUCT = 0; repeat M2 times: PRODUCT = PRODUCT + M1\n#     ANSWER = 0;  repeat M3 times: ANSWER = ANSWER + PRODUCT\n# The sizes are small (they add up to at most the number of boxes), so counting is quick enough.\n@ TOP_DONE\n\tlia 0; putA PRODUCT; putA PRODUCT_H                          # PRODUCT = 0\n\tcopy M2 TIMES                                                # TIMES = M2\n@ FIRST_PRODUCT\n\tgetA TIMES; lib 0                                            # A = TIMES, B = 0\n\tif_eq FIRST_PRODUCT_DONE                                     # M1 has been added M2 times\n\tlib 1; sub; putA TIMES                                       # TIMES = TIMES - 1\n\tget32A PRODUCT; getB M1; sum; put32A PRODUCT                 # PRODUCT = PRODUCT + M1\n\tgoto FIRST_PRODUCT\n@ FIRST_PRODUCT_DONE\n\tcopy M3 TIMES                                                # TIMES = M3   (ANSWER is 0 from the start)\n@ SECOND_PRODUCT\n\tgetA TIMES; lib 0                                            # A = TIMES, B = 0\n\tif_eq WRITE_ANSWER                                           # PRODUCT has been added M3 times\n\tlib 1; sub; putA TIMES                                       # TIMES = TIMES - 1\n\tget32AB ANSWER PRODUCT; sum; put32A ANSWER                   # ANSWER = ANSWER + PRODUCT\n\tgoto SECOND_PRODUCT\n\n# ==== write ANSWER as 10 decimal digits ====\n# A digit is how often a power of ten fits into what is left. Without division that is counting:\n#     REST = ANSWER\n#     for POWER in 10^9, 10^8, ..., 10, 1:                  # the table ends at a lower word of 0\n#         DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n#         while REST >= POWER: REST = REST - POWER; DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n#         memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE; DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n# (The lower word of a power of ten up to 10^15 is never 0, so a 0 there can end the table.)\n@ WRITE_ANSWER\n\tcopy ANSWER REST; copy ANSWER_H REST_H                       # REST = ANSWER\n@ NEXT_POWER\n\tgetAat POW                                                   # A = the lower word of the next power of ten\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq END                                                    # a lower word of 0 ends the table\n\tputA POWER                                                   # POWER = the power of ten; lower word\n\tgetAoff POW 1; putA POWER_H                                  # upper word\n\tgetA POW; lib 2; sum; putA POW                               # POW = POW + 2\n\tlia 0; putA DIGIT_VALUE                                      # DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n@ FITS_AGAIN\n\tget32AB REST POWER; sub                                      # A = REST - POWER\n\tif_carry FITS                                                # REST >= POWER: it fits once more\n\tgetA DIGIT_VALUE; putAat DIGIT                               # memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE\n\tinc DIGIT                                                    # DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n\tgoto NEXT_POWER\n@ FITS\n\tput32A REST                                                  # REST = REST - POWER   (A still holds the difference)\n\tinc DIGIT_VALUE                                              # DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n\tgoto FITS_AGAIN\n\n# The word 0 stops the CPU on its Breakpoint.\n@ END\n\tnon\n\n# ---- Subroutines ----\norigin 0x1800\n# ADD_SQUARE: D2 = D2 + S * S, for S below 32,768, with the table of squares.\n# Write S = H * 256 + L. Then S * S = H*H * 65536 + 2*H*L * 256 + L*L, and 2*H*L = (H+L)*(H+L) - H*H - L*L.\n# H is below 128 and L below 256, so all three squares are in the table (H + L is at most 382).\n#     H = S / 256; L = S mod 256                            # S * 256 in A: H is the upper half, L * 256 the lower\n#     SH = SQ[H]; SL = SQ[L]\n#     A = (SH * 256 + SQ[H + L] - SH - SL) * 256 + SL\n#     D2 = D2 + A\n@ ADD_SQUARE\n\tgetA S; shf | 8                                              # A = S * 256\n\tlib H; store rgAH rgB                                        # H = the upper half of A = S / 256\n\tlib L; store rgA rgB                                         # L = the lower half of A = (S mod 256) * 256, for now\n\tgetA L; shf | 8; lib L; store rgAH rgB                       # L = S mod 256 (shifted on, it is the upper half)\n\tgetA H; getB SQLO; sum; load rgA rgA; putA SH                # SH = SQ[H], which fits one word\n\tgetA L; getB SQLO; sum; load rgA rgA; putA SL                # SL = SQ[L], which fits one word\n\tgetA SH; shf | 8; psh rgAH; psh rgA                          # SH * 256 goes on the stack\n\tgetA H; getB L; sum; putA HL                                 # HL = H + L\n\tgetB SQLO; sum; putA ADR                                     # ADR = the address of the lower word of SQ[HL]\n\tgetA HL; getB SQHI; sum; load rgA rgA; shf | 8; shf | 8      # A = the upper word of SQ[HL], moved into the upper half\n\tgetB ADR; load rgA rgB                                       # the lower half of A = the lower word: A = SQ[HL]\n\tgetB SH; sub; getB SL; sub                                   # A = SQ[HL] - SH - SL = 2 * H * L\n\tpop rgB; pop rgBH; sum                                       # A = SH * 256 + 2 * H * L (B = SH * 256 from the stack)\n\tshf | 8; getB SL; sum                                        # A = that * 256 + SL = S * S\n\tpsh rgAH; psh rgA; get32A D2; pop rgB; pop rgBH              # A = D2, B = S * S (by way of the stack)\n\tsum; put32A D2                                               # D2 = D2 + S * S\n\tret\n\n# FIND: F = the root of the circuit of the box at F.\n#     while PARENT[F] != F: F = PARENT[F]\n@ FIND\n\tgetA F; getB TO_PARENT; sum; load rgA rgA                    # A = PARENT[F], the word at F + 0x1800\n\tgetB F                                                       # B = F\n\tif_eq FIND_DONE                                              # F is its own parent: the root\n\tputA F                                                       # F = PARENT[F]\n\tgoto FIND\n@ FIND_DONE\n\tret\n\n# ---- Powers of ten for the decimal digits: 10^9 down to 1, two words each, lower first; then 0 ----\norigin 0x1E00\n0xCA00; 0x3B9A    # 1000000000\n0xE100; 0x05F5    # 100000000\n0x9680; 0x0098    # 10000000\n0x4240; 0x000F    # 1000000\n0x86A0; 0x0001    # 100000\n0x2710; 0x0000    # 10000\n0x03E8; 0x0000    # 1000\n0x0064; 0x0000    # 100\n0x000A; 0x0000    # 10\n0x0001; 0x0000    # 1\n0\n\n# ---- Powers of two for SELECT: 2^29 down to 1, two words each, lower first; then 0, 0 ----\norigin 0x1E40\n0x0000; 0x2000    # 2^29\n0x0000; 0x1000    # 2^28\n0x0000; 0x0800    # 2^27\n0x0000; 0x0400    # 2^26\n0x0000; 0x0200    # 2^25\n0x0000; 0x0100    # 2^24\n0x0000; 0x0080    # 2^23\n0x0000; 0x0040    # 2^22\n0x0000; 0x0020    # 2^21\n0x0000; 0x0010    # 2^20\n0x0000; 0x0008    # 2^19\n0x0000; 0x0004    # 2^18\n0x0000; 0x0002    # 2^17\n0x0000; 0x0001    # 2^16\n0x8000; 0x0000    # 2^15\n0x4000; 0x0000    # 2^14\n0x2000; 0x0000    # 2^13\n0x1000; 0x0000    # 2^12\n0x0800; 0x0000    # 2^11\n0x0400; 0x0000    # 2^10\n0x0200; 0x0000    # 2^9\n0x0100; 0x0000    # 2^8\n0x0080; 0x0000    # 2^7\n0x0040; 0x0000    # 2^6\n0x0020; 0x0000    # 2^5\n0x0010; 0x0000    # 2^4\n0x0008; 0x0000    # 2^3\n0x0004; 0x0000    # 2^2\n0x0002; 0x0000    # 2^1\n0x0001; 0x0000    # 2^0\n0; 0\n\n# ---- The gaps of the sort, then 0 ----\norigin 0x1E80\n364; 121; 40; 13; 4; 1; 0\n\n# ---- The boxes: 1000, then the end mark ----\n# Six words per box: X, Y, Z, each lower word first.\norigin 0x2000\n0x70A7; 0x0000; 0x4122; 0x0000; 0x5AD9; 0x0001    # 28839,16674,88793\n0x71EA; 0x0000; 0x296F; 0x0000; 0xB7AE; 0x0000    # 29162,10607,47022\n0xE40C; 0x0000; 0x731F; 0x0000; 0xB6B4; 0x0000    # 58380,29471,46772\n0xD2BD; 0x0000; 0x4654; 0x0001; 0x366A; 0x0000    # 53949,83540,13930